-Similarity-based classification methods determine optical properties by measuring the distance or correlation between a sample point's feature vector and a set of reference exemplars, annotations, or latent representations, rather than relying on absolute scalar thresholds [S1][S2][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. This approach solves the problem of segmenting complex, high-dimensional, or semantically rich data where local intensity values are ambiguous or insufficient for material separation [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on geometric derivatives (e.g., [Curvature-based Transfer Function](5452-curvature-based-transfer-function.md)) or statistical histograms ([Local Histogram-based Classification Probability](14137-local-histogram-based-classification-probability.md)), similarity-based methods leverage global or learned relational structures to group voxels with similar semantic or spectral characteristics [S1][S4, unofficial].
+Similarity-based classification methods determine optical properties by measuring the distance or correlation between a sample point's feature vector and a set of reference exemplars, annotations, or latent representations, rather than relying solely on local scalar intensity or gradient magnitude [S1][S4, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. This approach solves the problem of semantic segmentation in high-dimensional or complex volumetric data where local features are ambiguous, by leveraging global structural or statistical similarity [S1]. It is distinguished from basic intensity-based classification by its ability to group voxels based on learned or user-defined semantic relationships (e.g., spectral signatures, latent space geometry) rather than raw value thresholds [S1][S4, unofficial].
 
