 ### [Range-Space Segmentation](13745-range-space-segmentation.md)
-Range-Space Segmentation is a method for visualizing and analyzing function field data by projecting high-dimensional functions at each spatial location into scalar fields based on their distance to a set of user-defined exemplar functions. It solves the problem of intelligently segmenting complex datasets (such as hyperspectral imagery or time-varying pollution data) by creating a classification field that assigns each spatial point to the nearest exemplar in function space. Unlike Multidimensional Scaling (MDS), which loses spatial layout, this method preserves the original spatial structure of the data while enabling direct volume rendering or isosurfacing of feature boundaries.
+Range-Space Segmentation is a method for visualizing and analyzing function field data by projecting high-dimensional function samples into scalar fields based on their distance to a set of user-defined exemplar functions. It solves the problem of intelligently segmenting complex datasets (such as hyperspectral imagery or time-varying pollution data) by creating a classification field that assigns each spatial point to the nearest exemplar, thereby enabling feature-based visualization and surface extraction. Unlike multidimensional scaling (MDS) techniques which lose spatial layout, this method preserves the original spatial structure of the data while reducing the functional dimensionality to a scalar distance field and an integer label field.
 
 ### [Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA)](14970-representational-similarity-analysis-rsa.md)
-Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) evaluates the relational structure (geometry of the manifold) induced over a set of views, complementing pointwise similarity measures. It assesses whether a reconstructed latent space captures the same representational organization as a ground-truth space by correlating their pairwise similarity structures. Unlike pointwise metrics that align individual codes, RSA ensures the global geometry of the manifold is preserved.
+Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA) is a method for evaluating the quality of a learned latent space by comparing the relational structure (manifold geometry) of two sets of representations against each other. Unlike pointwise similarity measures that assess individual latent codes in isolation, RSA assesses whether the pairwise similarity structure across an entire set of views is preserved. It is primarily used in the context of self-supervised learning and differentiable rendering to verify if a reconstructed latent space captures the same representational organization as a ground-truth or reference space.
 
 ### Cosine Similarity Map Computation
-**Definition:** Computes a similarity volume $S_L$ by comparing annotated feature vectors against the full feature volume $F$ using cosine similarity, enabling interactive feedback [S1].
-**Formula:**
-$$ S_L^C = \max_{a \in A_C} \frac{1}{|F|} \sum_{i \in F} \frac{F_a \cdot F_i}{\|F_a\|_2 \|F_i\|_2} $$
-where $A_C$ is the set of annotations for class $C$, $F_a$ is the feature vector at annotation $a$, and $F_i$ is the feature vector at voxel $i$. Resulting $S_L^C \in [0, 1]^{W' \times H' \times D'}$ [S1].
-To select spatially local structures, the similarity map is scaled by a proximity parameter $\tilde{p} \in [0, 1]$:
-$$ P(x) = \max_{a \in A_C} e^{-10\tilde{p}|x-a|} $$
-where $x$ is the location in $\mathbb{R}^3$ and $a$ is an annotation location [S1].
-**Parameters:** Proximity parameter $\tilde{p} \in [0, 1]$ [S1].
-**Performance:** Computation time is a few milliseconds on CPU or GPU [S1].
